#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157002 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "2a284986-5300-5bad-a33c-aa2bc47712b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7043" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/1ee7ef0a95e618121f4e0653c6beaa64248682ab524ecee8903acec89827e77d.png)\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/031dd658d772ef4184ceb173311102f64bd0ff3ff5e34321ad4648e0d5026383.png)\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/d82f994f6426a091fb6fc46b2b2b6fbc66d0b94e7ed1ead3e67257292f4c985b.png)*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/b1ba76ba62019524549b4bcf4c36ec2440b2db8f585150a9456218972363a215.png) \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/f86bdf3e241a51548f8c58fe739e11071197944f5fe2361f83bb34408333211c.png)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/9b2628d7d3c88303bdd7ed2a8aadcdcfe751a01d910f64972a60cc080d039181.png)\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t) = \\cos{t} + \\sin{t}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157003 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "8e797f4b-fe53-5d0a-a4b0-8fb51ee04c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-7044" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/4d932848b1d30448dd0135bc75ded935f2065037d4964423899013a47c7a526a.png)\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/a474b7882cfa32e343d1781a731a4eb540994f72730320d7b8a61639e7e86a14.png)*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/60dd7b0b483c745b0ffda2c89d3216ab463cab5870f91b58b54c7f8e4e446cfd.png)\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/9e4e860107360ac18ce72e7f4f07dbf9106de041dea3b6c9228cc30bc45c714e.png)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/175d9bde8dda1bfb3a653521add06d23944d72019c0718eed6407abe97f2e87c.png)\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}](\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/12f33df5f036901c6069ee03a9ba8ccb05cde1071b7add3d1b310da12bb8298d.png) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3692fa3-14c5-5db9-8fee-e99ae35e386f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "53243f33-77a1-59f1-bce9-3f084e087593" :question/math-academy-id "e-7045" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/ae6acacc8b2c54be234e536c373c40f4b5b53646fe6091181672c06e437f5366.png)\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/14c8e142a4fc541b8da91c6bcd1c8517451b436b7f8c677bfb239ca4bfa79588.png)\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $\\lambda_1=1+\\textrm{i}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/471ff0aa856363c49a494f0e711dec8558b429261ddd99685bd0a19416264d05.png) \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=1+\\textrm{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For $\\lambda_1 =1+\\textrm{i},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/9fbccda402b330b568828c47040f1867c0b075ff434f6d94c2d5d5bcea85f4a8.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/91e10c00aed02305fdb5afd421aad57fe52143e3305d84da00819e62f2b30a14.png)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 1+\\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/2ccc9c4cb7dd95e168690c017e8fca6f92315d1759b3e3ec6bb3a82551b39e3c.png)\n\nWe now separate this solution into its real and imaginary parts. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ } \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t) = \\cos{t}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1633293-c176-5bfe-8fa9-cfd872ceca26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "9bd3af1e-e569-5cf1-8c4e-97b9ecc35174" :question/math-academy-id "e-7046" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/082bb18706b8bae20dbc5b8531829f3b8523d9262c2fd0f7c4be78a3b2ef4ebb.png)\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $\\lambda_1=3+\\textrm{i}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/657dced55da3afc2c15487267793aaf282e8c258ca5e7556bacecff58a496d82.png) \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=3+\\textrm{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For $\\lambda_1 =3+\\textrm{i},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/9fbccda402b330b568828c47040f1867c0b075ff434f6d94c2d5d5bcea85f4a8.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/5d9c13b213c1e44a755000997c61f5ba5a3c91159eb7a5e6a574acf34019efab.png)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 3 + \\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/38fd775722fd8ce56abb0f09499bb0a268ae73b9483a182d4564381557d190cf.png)\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(3+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}({e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/f2282999a9f221bd9a14118c116c573f82521e6140d24b008b6fea04c5a61fcf.png) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-3[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb8ca786-181e-5ecf-beff-8e25eddb9628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (2t) + \\cos (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155250} {:answer/id #uuid "c288fe2f-745f-57cb-a1a2-a70d3f56e432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin (2t) - \\cos (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155251} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fde839-791f-51ee-bc3e-848e027d3a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (2t) - 3\\cos (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155252} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5185a1-4f47-559a-b1ea-55a06d244647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin (2t) - 3\\cos (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155253} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcaa4f9-6725-5c7c-a944-c4fe34d5f5cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (2t) + 2\\cos (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dcaa4f9-6725-5c7c-a944-c4fe34d5f5cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (2t) + 2\\cos (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155254} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ee3f7bc-1314-58f0-9d28-f8ac998f3ed4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "284bef67-027d-5606-82aa-6c0a65dd3c2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-328592" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3692fa3-14c5-5db9-8fee-e99ae35e386f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81122cc2-d3f0-50d2-b42a-650f2c9e839b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin (2t) + \\cos (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155268} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0b5229-d8bc-5aef-914f-e1cd0a6d7e13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155269} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8452da-04ab-5cde-bcef-ff41a16c4315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (2t) - 2\\cos (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155270} {:answer/id #uuid "119fbf02-c2c9-5e4c-bd70-fd8387f9843f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155271} {:answer/id #uuid "2e32f9fe-e7bc-5203-b231-a9e28d93fb72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sin (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e32f9fe-e7bc-5203-b231-a9e28d93fb72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sin (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155272} :answer-field/id #uuid "71b78ff0-9861-57e3-8a76-7fec68999d4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f547dfe-7bb2-5104-89c7-1b8cdce6b850" :question/math-academy-id "q-328595" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+2\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(1+2\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(1+2\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}(2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7e73afe-f47a-5b3f-8bca-a08c6153155d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7e73afe-f47a-5b3f-8bca-a08c6153155d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356208} :answer-field/id #uuid "a91cd0d8-2cd7-56af-abaa-ed476de7883a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155291} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba5b6ac7-3494-5685-a59a-1216275d474f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cos(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba5b6ac7-3494-5685-a59a-1216275d474f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cos(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356209} :answer-field/id #uuid "e24a9c33-94d6-5f76-b1bc-2ceba0b7ee6a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2ec92c3-9f21-5a42-ab9d-d5296a6ec794" :question/math-academy-id "q-329578" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}g(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}.$*\n\n$f(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,$\n\n$g(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ca793e9-bc51-51e0-b5d6-f10b57417348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356212} {:answer/id #uuid "e769de61-ae72-5dc1-8e89-72f5e6e6fffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356213} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2a365b-cac6-5320-bbd4-259278db53f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356214} {:answer/id #uuid "044f666a-47cc-5419-ad8f-94e26c6e84fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356215} {:answer/id #uuid "23693746-303c-56f3-8525-7b1d9afc7b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e769de61-ae72-5dc1-8e89-72f5e6e6fffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356213} :answer-field/id #uuid "67f015e1-faaf-5163-aa87-0199e5580cae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "612040bc-4542-522a-a1e4-90bc0e943d0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333908" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a013a67e-7155-59f7-bfda-597aac7c721c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356219} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9fa2e0-c352-553a-bcdf-b84397a86a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356220} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d45aa1-73af-5f49-a944-43fff8309bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356221} {:answer/id #uuid "5c35146c-a057-5a87-8762-4aa0238ef72b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356222} {:answer/id #uuid "725214f9-98b2-58ed-91ce-3b929cbef666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c35146c-a057-5a87-8762-4aa0238ef72b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356222} :answer-field/id #uuid "556a23a3-a2ba-5074-90e1-f53ba2e6402c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7848e39-87f6-5f74-a37b-941df8c79a27" :question/math-academy-id "q-333909" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61c5d913-d230-57d2-8fa0-dabfd34182a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sin(12t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356226} {:answer/id #uuid "34a982aa-2379-5160-aadb-0e221bbe01c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin(12t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34a982aa-2379-5160-aadb-0e221bbe01c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin(12t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356227} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e92f80b-24f0-5a62-95f7-57ffb2c0b02c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356225} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c60452b-ee5e-5c06-bfaa-b6e3aa419816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\cos(12t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c60452b-ee5e-5c06-bfaa-b6e3aa419816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\cos(12t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356229} :answer-field/id #uuid "449749b2-fc95-5450-b01f-b347f4801903" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57268f0c-de26-5f91-9b87-6de7da62058e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333916" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 18 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}g(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}.$*\n\n$f(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,g(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 18 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-12\\text{i} & 18 \\\\ -8 & -12\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{12i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{12i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nf(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t),\\,g(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da01089c-2bba-5fe0-9926-0b166dc152f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356232} {:answer/id #uuid "36f69d04-eeb0-528a-8966-bdea6d6df63a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356233} {:answer/id #uuid "b40cfc1a-435b-5da5-9492-641be7200e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356234} {:answer/id #uuid "3aef1efb-5d13-592b-b3d6-895d84479414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356235} {:answer/id #uuid "6285f3bf-8b72-51ca-86c1-18e0cdfe8c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36f69d04-eeb0-528a-8966-bdea6d6df63a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356233} :answer-field/id #uuid "de7d054f-4d3f-52c6-9324-255ff9cd28c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f26b8b6-ea22-55f9-adb9-e96204409f88" :question/math-academy-id "q-334053" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 10 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 10 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}](\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f9524f-fb76-5225-ab27-74448861f246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356239} {:answer/id #uuid "0b54f964-32d3-5846-bdfd-e84deecaca9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356240} {:answer/id #uuid "2036f215-1611-5a69-802b-f72da7ed8cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356241} {:answer/id #uuid "bea3e524-a5a4-5f89-aeff-25f9d1bc7c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356242} {:answer/id #uuid "7258b1b7-265f-595d-bece-46ef942ce588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7258b1b7-265f-595d-bece-46ef942ce588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356243} :answer-field/id #uuid "685eefa4-ce1b-55af-af15-3d4f19cb51f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b38a33fe-2eac-50c5-b2e0-e3e2d3c1ac54" :question/math-academy-id "q-334054" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}](\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =3[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072601ab-6c31-5585-89f9-7f5891cc48f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356246} {:answer/id #uuid "76c23972-a758-5ee2-9439-dad8dc1f5048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356247} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee210e0-bfaf-5a6a-b298-076886e9bf32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356248} {:answer/id #uuid "9483f09e-3581-5707-a7ab-a2e1e2443d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356249} {:answer/id #uuid "bf133430-e75b-5d8d-8a9c-b21fea7dd6a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "072601ab-6c31-5585-89f9-7f5891cc48f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356246} :answer-field/id #uuid "b247e3cc-ef1d-5a19-9b3a-6b7b15f98372" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "048c0890-2cea-5141-b151-d83b632a790f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334058" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ 2{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =0[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "900b9aea-357c-5830-ab35-28bd6e1b02ec" :