x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "854d9ef9-627c-5e93-a220-59d0696580b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356260} {:answer/id #uuid "32771a8f-2a90-52bb-bad4-edbd7c8e08a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356261} {:answer/id #uuid "0787c17e-bc9f-56cf-8abc-1c5d2f310d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356262} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a42a7c-7ebc-5f79-bb00-8c23700f11a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356263} {:answer/id #uuid "60fe351a-c39a-5367-a787-1633dbe35045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9a42a7c-7ebc-5f79-bb00-8c23700f11a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356263} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e29796e-67ad-5573-ba47-064742050095" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1230ef0c-2736-5f01-ab44-6913847462dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-334063" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 26 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-5\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 26 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-5\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-5\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1-5\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-5\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-5\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+0[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3692fa3-14c5-5db9-8fee-e99ae35e386f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7eae624b-642c-56e1-8ab6-3f08e764bfca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356267} {:answer/id #uuid "02711d17-ce3b-56a5-93fb-cb0086015f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356268} {:answer/id #uuid "541da5ac-12a0-54e5-8b6c-d4629508aaa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356269} {:answer/id #uuid "6dbb493c-1a93-5b05-aea1-fb71ade265ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356270} {:answer/id #uuid "42a63f48-72c6-5731-9f79-2e1f9bb6009b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dbb493c-1a93-5b05-aea1-fb71ade265ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356270} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c0633a-1d2c-5d91-97ad-35dd51c82fd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f345db86-4fa2-5aaf-98e1-0020feb0363b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334103" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ f(t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+\\text{i}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(4+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(4+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\text{i}({e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3692fa3-14c5-5db9-8fee-e99ae35e386f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a055744-d194-50cf-88db-4c0a8291afcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356274} {:answer/id #uuid "475f9169-0a25-53c6-ad09-23813f5c3aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356275} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b72144-ce21-559a-abf9-c196404e06f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356276} {:answer/id #uuid "f1193d21-e9d0-55cb-9004-9bd9728dde45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356277} {:answer/id #uuid "0e757361-d7c8-5315-bc9a-d223eb5207e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "475f9169-0a25-53c6-ad09-23813f5c3aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356275} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ef3c711-2aee-58dd-81a4-13a2a02d97fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24bfb8fa-479a-5ccb-9005-78c61251c83b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334104" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+2\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(1+2\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(1+2\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3692fa3-14c5-5db9-8fee-e99ae35e386f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a03c9f47-52dd-5cdc-96da-fed3ce763f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356281} {:answer/id #uuid "b1335884-5707-5068-bfd9-a238aeecb73d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356282} {:answer/id #uuid "f7849968-14ed-58e7-85ed-cf26a7dda75b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356283} {:answer/id #uuid "152e4961-88b5-53c4-8fab-fa9e8a0db282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356284} {:answer/id #uuid "41811ccf-5ef9-5bb1-a37c-b667071da03b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a03c9f47-52dd-5cdc-96da-fed3ce763f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356281} :answer-field/id #uuid "beeb7142-5036-541c-b720-27ed7b24b2e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75fa7e81-82d6-51b5-9f54-494e1c3e9275" :question/math-academy-id "q-334126" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -15 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+6\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -15 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+6\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+6\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(4+6\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(4+6\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+2\\text{i}{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-2{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t))+\\text{i}{e}^{4t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)) \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3692fa3-14c5-5db9-8fee-e99ae35e386f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f8092fa-ec29-5a5f-a16b-1b3d5c2f5ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356288} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc886f8-78c0-5945-a3a2-776d36fbc774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356289} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6ca26d-626e-5d10-8f1a-2fc7e57c138c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356290} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5f5b26-26f3-509a-a8f2-15b7158e094c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356291} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b41330-3318-5dee-b6e3-0bcea0c73869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a5f5b26-26f3-509a-a8f2-15b7158e094c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356291} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bba62ad-249a-5173-8607-ca989c48a568" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9ab2de5-aa72-577a-9ea4-5c1cf6201d82" :question/math-academy-id "q-334132" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6+3\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6+3\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6+3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(6+3\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(6+3\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\text{i}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {e}^{6t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+\\text{i}{e}^{6t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ {e}^{6t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1633293-c176-5bfe-8fa9-cfd872ceca26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d957078c-a9e9-5506-8ace-7ab5a7c99459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356295} {:answer/id #uuid "95c7ee83-4b45-5999-a533-f27188af044d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356296} {:answer/id #uuid "468e9a8d-c1cd-5822-bc51-466e75a50bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356297} {:answer/id #uuid "90875bc6-8fb2-58dd-94f2-de019f8b96b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356298} {:answer/id #uuid "2033906c-167a-589b-b889-a26025f05879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2033906c-167a-589b-b889-a26025f05879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356299} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc881e51-c33d-5f34-815a-c92f267de55e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64c852b4-5cf9-5308-a3bf-3d121c7ebf57" :question/math-academy-id "q-334214" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-2+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-2+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =2[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}-[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1633293-c176-5bfe-8fa9-cfd872ceca26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e3a89c4-cf37-5b83-a180-a9bc0a442dbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356302} {:answer/id #uuid "6bbf160e-0ede-5834-b08d-58ed51469228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356303} {:answer/id #uuid "d359fdcc-bd98-51e3-9a01-ad1eb3cf877c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356304} {:answer/id #uuid "cf71447c-f4ed-5b04-b2d4-96afbb5084fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356305} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2a1beb-a321-54dc-9cbc-3e56674ecd1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d359fdcc-bd98-51e3-9a01-ad1eb3cf877c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356304} :answer-field/id #uuid "aca6858e-fbdc-5231-b205-54cdd5604b33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fc6ee3c-e10e-57cd-92e7-361a4aae8ac6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334223" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(3+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(3+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\text{i}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-3[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1633293-c176-5bfe-8fa9-cfd872ceca26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7b64d48-d290-5a18-9dbf-c9813f57cca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356309} {:answer/id #uuid "1792d260-a87f-54bb-b7fc-37cbaf1905ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356310} {:answer/id #uuid "b124e3bf-f629-524b-80fc-9edbcf48b54f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356311} {:answer/id #uuid "a258ba24-a26c-5959-8879-f10880f53826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356312} {:answer/id #uuid "631f1e1a-f969-5eff-88c2-88ae07b11a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1792d260-a87f-54bb-b7fc-37cbaf1905ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356310} :answer-field/id #uuid "788d4082-a56e-581f-a987-56b7770c403d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb5e5230-b38a-5e0c-a077-5495818362fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334224" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(2+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(2+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}(3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}(3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-2[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+0[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1633293-c176-5bfe-8fa9-cfd872ceca26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89fb594f-b3cf-5efa-b89e-97c9e8673d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356316} {:answer/id #uuid "cc99503e-5c86-51a9-8ff3-16af132b6c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356317} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b49a0f-b7fe-51c2-8979-98b6d41875a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356318} {:answer/id #uuid "2513c26c-b1f6-55a9-a9c1-99829a57e2c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356319} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe966e8-92a2-507f-8bc2-60de21bdaeb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2513c26c-b1f6-55a9-a9c1-99829a57e2c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356319} :answer-field/id #uuid "43068ed1-c078-540f-bf2d-dbb42d7d8e64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49fdff55-2b59-5c9d-8ddb-e7f7a199ced4" :question/math-academy-id "q-334225" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+0[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1633293-c176-5bfe-8fa9-cfd872ceca26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1387da82-63aa-5399-bb0e-f3b38c6277fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356323} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9f50ad-404f-59a6-b175-265488098e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356324} {:answer/id #uuid "e9095dfa-c9d5-5aad-b15a-477d3ac1bc34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356325} {:answer/id #uuid "14be500f-2827-5b4c-b9ad-2c81336f0202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356326} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6b1397-0803-59ae-a01f-821c9f2c4698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1387da82-63aa-5399-bb0e-f3b38c6277fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356323} :answer-field/id #uuid "43fb31b0-1b04-50a5-a349-6e93da362218" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee07f5ea-042a-564e-aed4-ca57bb778d81" :question/math-academy-id "q-334226" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-3+2\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-3+2\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =2[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}-3[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
